userStories.description:
**Goal** — Implement the pure task half of flow-rulings.ts and export the existing oneLine helper unchanged.

**Motivation** — Live flows 7-9 (fleet design doc §9.174-§9.176) reach `FIX`, but a draft PR whose open items need rulings stops the flow `pr_not_ready`, and the human has to rule each item through `POST .../fleet/jobs/:id/rulings` and dispatch `FIX` by hand.

**Approach** — New pure module `apps/api/src/fleet/flows/flow-rulings.ts`:

`ruleTaskItems` keeps items whose kind is neither `finding` nor `ci` and whose id is not in `ruledItemIds`, in input order. `needsAutomaticRule` is true when any kept item's kind is in `FLOW_RULE_KINDS`.

`FLOW_RULING_INSTRUCTION` states, one line each: rule only the listed items; never rule a finding; advisor or ruling items: `verified: <evidence>` when the premise holds and nothing remains to change, `confirm: <evidence>` only for a code change `nax bot fix` can push and never for a PRD or acceptance-criteria edit, `override: <reason>` when the premise is wrong; `defer` is not a ruling; cannot-verify items: `verified: <evidence>` after running what it names, else `waive: <why it cannot be checked>`; story items: `waive: <reason citing the spec or PRD line>` when out of scope or accepted, no ruling for a code gap; reason items: `verified: <evidence>` or `waive: <reason>`; gate items: re-run `nax gates run --gate <name> --json`, `fixed <head sha>` only when it is green at the head, no ruling when red; ask a question only when the answer changes what gets built and the spec, PRD and code do not settle it; end with one JSON object on its own line, no text after it:
`{"rulings":[{"item":"<id>","ruling":"<keyword line and evidence>"}],"questions":[{"item":"<id>","question":"<question>"}]}`.

`buildRuleTask` returns: `Rule the open workflow items of PR #<n>.`, a blank line, then (guided only) a `Guidance from the user:` block with the held reason line `Still held: <held>` when present and one `Q<id>: <question> A: <answer>` line per answer, a blank line, then one line per item `- <id> (<kind>[, <severity>])[ <file>[:<line>]]: <issue>[ Suggestion: <suggestion>]`, then a blank line and `FLOW_RULING_INSTRUCTION`. Every interpolated item, question and answer text is collapsed to one line first, so an `issue` with newlines cannot forge item lines. Each `Q<id>: ... A: ...` line is cut to 300 characters and the `Still held:` line to 500, so 20 maximum-length answers still fit. Only item lines are cut when the task would exceed `AGENT_TASK_MAX_LENGTH`: whole item lines are dropped from the end and a final `(N more items not shown)` line is added, so the guidance and the instruction are always complete. An item with a `line` but no `file` shows no location.

`oneLine` (`apps/api/src/fleet/flows/flow-calls.ts:136`). Target: exported, reused by `buildRuleTask`. Its behaviour and callers are unchanged.

**Scope** — In: pure item selection, automatic-rule predicate, instruction, bounded task formatting, exporting the existing oneLine helper without changing its callers' behavior and co-located unit tests. Out: verdict parsing (US-002), dispatch (US-003), decisions (US-007/008), persistence and orchestration (US-005/010/011). A flow-side code fix that does not go through `FIX` (`nax bot fix`); RULE never pushes.

**Interface**
```ts
export type FlowOpenItem = { id: string; kind: string; issue: string; severity?: string | null;
  file?: string | null; line?: number | null; suggestion?: string | null };
export type FlowRuleGuidance = { held: string | null; questions: FlowQuestion[]; answers: FlowAnswer[] };
export const FLOW_RULE_KINDS = ['advisor', 'ruling', 'cannot-verify', 'story', 'reason'] as const;
export const FLOW_RULING_INSTRUCTION: string;
export function ruleTaskItems(items: FlowOpenItem[], ruledItemIds: string[]): FlowOpenItem[];
export function needsAutomaticRule(items: FlowOpenItem[], ruledItemIds: string[]): boolean;
export function buildRuleTask(input: { prNumber: number; items: FlowOpenItem[]; guidance: FlowRuleGuidance | null }): string;
```

**Goal** — Add the pure RULE verdict parser, result types and ruling count limit in flow-rulings.ts.

**Motivation** — Arc A (#390) already lets an agent rule an item and a `FIX` apply the rulings.

**Approach** — `parseRuleVerdict` reads the last line that starts with `{"rulings"` and parses it as JSON, tolerating a single trailing code-fence line after it as `parseFlowVerdict` does. `rulings` and `questions` must be arrays (either may be empty); each ruling needs non-blank string `item` and `ruling`, each question non-blank `item` and `question`; anything else, other trailing text after the object, or no such line is `{ ok: false }`. Each returned ruling text passes through `sanitizeFlowText` with U+0000 also replaced by U+FFFD, then is cut to 2,000 characters (the `parseRulingText` limit); each question text the same, cut to `FLOW_CALL_TEXT_MAX` (1,000), so no agent text can break a JSON column. More than `FLOW_RULINGS_MAX` rulings is `{ ok: false }`.

This story has no dependencies. US-001 authors the task half in the same module and co-located spec; contribute the parser half additively and preserve it regardless of execution order. Do not impose a dependency on US-001. The new parser requires a marker at the start of a line, even though the existing parseFlowVerdict uses lastIndexOf without that restriction.

**Scope** — In: verdict types, shape validation, trailing-fence compatibility, count limit and JSON-safe text bounds. Out: task construction (US-001), decision/question handling (US-007), ruling eligibility and storage (US-005), orchestration (US-010).

**Interface**
```ts
export type FlowRuling = { item: string; ruling: string };
export type FlowRuleQuestion = { item: string; question: string };
export const FLOW_RULINGS_MAX = 100;
export function parseRuleVerdict(output: string | null):
  | { ok: true; rulings: FlowRuling[]; questions: FlowRuleQuestion[] } | { ok: false };
```

**Goal** — Implement createForFlow, ruledItemIds and commentRefused using Arc A eligibility and system-on-behalf-of attribution.

**Motivation** — Live flows 7-9 (fleet design doc §9.174-§9.176) reach `FIX`, but a draft PR whose open items need rulings stops the flow `pr_not_ready`, and the human has to rule each item through `POST .../fleet/jobs/:id/rulings` and dispatch `FIX` by hand.

**Approach** — `FleetPrRulingsService.createForFlow`:

First, when `latestRuling(runJobId, itemId)` is `pending`, `actorType: 'SYSTEM'` and has the same trimmed text, it returns `{ kind: 'recorded', rulingId: <that id> }` with no writes. Otherwise, outside any transaction it runs `ruleTarget` then `assertItemIndex`; a thrown app exception becomes `{ kind: 'refused', reason: <its message key> }`. Then, under `runAsSystem('flow', fn, { onBehalfOf: creator })`, in one transaction: `lockTarget(projectSlug, runJobId)`, `ruleTarget` again (refusal -> `refused`), load the creator's principal with `ActorPrincipalLoader` (unavailable -> `refused`, reason `creator unavailable`), `ensureFinishTicket`, `createSystemRulingComment(ticketId, '<item> (<kind>): <text> (flow <flowId>)')`, `replacePending`, `insert` with `actorType: 'SYSTEM'`, `actorId: 'flow'`, `approvalRequestId` from the input, and one audit record `system.flow.advance` with summary `flow ruled <item>: <disposition>`.

**No new permission or approval.** The in-process ruling path skips the route gate: the flow's approval covers it. It still runs every eligibility check of `ruleTarget` and the item-index check of `assertItemIndex`.

**Recording is idempotent.** `createForFlow` returns the existing ruling, with no new comment, audit or insert, when the item's latest ruling is `pending`, `SYSTEM`-authored and has the same text, so a tick that crashed after some rulings re-runs safely.

**The item-index check runs per ruling** (`assertItemIndex`, at most `FLOW_RULINGS_MAX` per RULE job); caching it is left out.

`FleetPrRulingsService` constructor (`apps/api/src/fleet/rulings/fleet-pr-rulings.service.ts:25`). Target: gains `ActorPrincipalLoader` as its last parameter.

`FleetPrRulingsService` (same file). Target: adds `createForFlow(input: FlowRulingInput): Promise<FlowRulingOutcome>` `ruledItemIds(runJobId: string): Promise<string[]>` (the item ids of `ruledItemIds` above, from `repo.list(runJobId)`'s newest row per item), and `commentRefused(runId: string, body: string): Promise<void>` delegating to `FleetJobTicketEffects.commentOnRulingResult`, so the advance service needs no second new dependency.

The protected item ids are items of that RUN whose latest ruling is `applied`, or `pending` from a `USER` or `AGENT`, so a flow never replaces a human's pending ruling. Preserve `ruleTarget`, `assertItemIndex`, `latestRunForPr`, repository locking/insertion helpers and parseRulingText unchanged. Unknown operational failures propagate instead of being mislabeled refused. Add the ActorPrincipalLoader stub as the final positional constructor argument in fleet-pr-rulings.service.spec.ts, fleet-pr-rulings.snapshot.spec.ts, fleet-pr-rulings.preflight.spec.ts, fleet-pr-rulings.results.spec.ts and tickets/finish-ticket.spec.ts; existing assertions are unchanged. Add new writer tests separately within the existing service spec harness without changing old expectations.

**Scope** — In: secure attribution, principal loading, refusal outcomes, idempotent atomic writer, protected latest-ruling ids, refusal-comment delegation and preservation of the existing direct create caller attribution. Out: SYSTEM representation primitive (US-004), task membership filtering and batch activity (US-010), flow progress marker (US-006). A new permission key or approval for the RULE step or the flow's rulings. Caching the item-index forge read across the rulings of one RULE job.

**Interface**
```ts
export type FlowRulingInput = { projectId: string; projectSlug: string; runJobId: string; itemId: string; text: string;
  creator: { type: 'USER' | 'AGENT'; id: string }; approvalRequestId: string | null; flowId: string };
export type FlowRulingOutcome = { kind: 'recorded'; rulingId: string } | { kind: 'refused'; reason: string };
createForFlow(input: FlowRulingInput): Promise<FlowRulingOutcome>;
ruledItemIds(runJobId: string): Promise<string[]>;
commentRefused(runId: string, body: string): Promise<void>;
```

**Goal** — Extend the pure decision contract and REVIEW/RULE branches without changing earlier steps.

**Motivation** — The flow design (`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-04-fleet-flow-design.md`, Steps, step 2.5) says the finish is "Arc A's rulings by agent, then the ready step"; Arc A (#390) already lets an agent rule an item and a `FIX` apply the rulings.

**Approach** — `FlowStepInput` (`apps/api/src/fleet/flows/flow-step-decision.ts:23`). Target: adds OPTIONAL `run?: { outcome: string | null; openItems: FlowOpenItem[] } | null` (the feature's latest `RUN` job's `workflow`), `ruledItemIds?: string[]` (items of that RUN whose latest ruling is `applied`, or `pending` from a `USER` or `AGENT`, so a flow never replaces a human's pending ruling) and `rulingsJobId?: string | null` (the `RULE` job whose rulings were recorded); absent reads as `null` / `[]`, so `decisionInput` compiles before US-011 fills them.

`FlowStepAction` (same file :53). Target: the `dispatch` variant gains optional `rule?: { answeredJobId: string | null }`; a new variant `{ kind: 'apply_rulings'; jobId: string; rulings: FlowRuling[] }`; `wait_answer.step` widens to `'SPEC_REVIEW' | 'PRD_REVIEW' | 'RULE' | 'FIX'`. Export ApplyRulingsAction as the extracted action type for US-010.

`decideFlowStep` PR-phase rules (single-feature flows; all earlier rules unchanged):
1. `REVIEW` COMPLETED, `featurePrNumber` null -> `wait` (unchanged).
2. `REVIEW` COMPLETED: `run?.outcome === 'draft'` and `needsAutomaticRule(run.openItems, ruledItemIds)` -> `dispatch RULE` with `rule: { answeredJobId: null }`; otherwise `dispatch FIX` (unchanged shape).
3. `RULE` COMPLETED, verdict unreadable -> stop `verdict_unreadable`, detail `RULE job <id>: no rulings JSON in the agent output`.
4. `RULE` with questions: `canApplyCalls` false -> stop `review_ambiguous` (detail lists the questions); more than `FLOW_CALLS_MAX_FINDINGS` -> stop `review_ambiguous`; `questionsJobId === job.id && answers !== null` -> `dispatch RULE` with `rule: { answeredJobId: job.id }`; otherwise `wait_answer` step `RULE` with positional questions whose text is `<item>: <question>`.
5. `RULE` with no questions: `rulingsJobId === job.id` -> `dispatch FIX` with the feature PR (`wait` while `featurePrNumber` is null); otherwise `apply_rulings` with the job id and the verdict's rulings (possibly empty). A verdict with both rulings and questions follows rule 4 (its rulings are not recorded).

**Questions take precedence over rulings in one verdict.** A RULE verdict with both is a wait: its rulings are not recorded, and the guided RULE after the answer rules again with the answers in hand.

**Questions from RULE** use the F4b path: positional ids, at most `FLOW_CALLS_MAX_FINDINGS` (20); more is a stop. An answered `RULE` question, like an answered `FIX` hold, dispatches a new guided `RULE`; answers are never turned into rulings directly, so every ruling keeps the agent's evidence.

RULE job FAILED / CANCELLED / CRASHED / ESCALATED -> stop `step_failed` (existing rule). Retain multi-feature skip and all earlier behavior. Add REVIEW/RULE cases to flow-step-decision.spec.ts; the input() fixture is unchanged because new fields are optional, and existing assertions are unchanged.

**Scope** — In: optional inputs, new action variants, widened question-step type and rules 1-5, including failure-table terminal cases. Out: FIX rules 6-9 (US-008), question payload/parser widening (US-009), transaction/orchestration (US-010/011). Turning human answers directly into rulings; answers are guidance for a new RULE job. Multi-feature flows; `decideFlowStep` still skips them (F5).

**Interface**
```ts
run?: { outcome: string | null; openItems: FlowOpenItem[] } | null;
ruledItemIds?: string[];
rulingsJobId?: string | null;
// dispatch option
rule?: { answeredJobId: string | null };
// new action
{ kind: 'apply_rulings'; jobId: string; rulings: FlowRuling[] };
// wait_answer.step
'SPEC_REVIEW' | 'PRD_REVIEW' | 'RULE' | 'FIX';
```

**Goal** — Wire the ruling service into flow advance and record batch progress, refusals and activity safely.

**Motivation** — Live flows 7-9 (fleet design doc §9.174-§9.176) reach `FIX`, but a draft PR whose open items need rulings stops the flow `pr_not_ready`, and the human has to rule each item through `POST .../fleet/jobs/:id/rulings` and dispatch `FIX` by hand.

**Approach** — `FleetFlowAdvanceService` constructor (`apps/api/src/fleet/flows/fleet-flow-advance.service.ts:48`). Target: gains `FleetPrRulingsService` as its last parameter; `FlowsModule` imports `FleetRulingsModule` (no cycle: it is already reached through `FleetJobsModule`).

`DispatchPass` (`apps/api/src/fleet/flows/fleet-flow-advance.service.ts:38`). Target: the type parameter also admits `ApplyRulingsAction`, and `advance()` branches on `apply_rulings` before reading `action.step` (US-010).

`apply_rulings` leaves the decide transaction like `apply_calls`; `advanceRulings` re-reads the RUN with `steps.latestJobForStep(flowId, 'RUN')`, `rulings.ruledItemIds(run.id)` and the slug with `steps.projectSlug`, and keeps only the last ruling per item id when the verdict repeats an item; (creator-access check first, no cost-cap check: rulings are not billed). `advanceRulings` calls `createForFlow` once per ruling, in verdict order, outside any transaction, skipping (as refused, reason `not in task`) any ruling whose item is not in `ruleTaskItems(run.openItems, ruledItemIds)`; then in one transaction under `lockLive` (nothing is written when the flow is no longer live): `recordRulings(flowId, featureId, ruleJobId)`, one `flow.rulings` activity record with payload `{ jobId, recorded: <item ids>, refused: [{ item, reason }] }`, and, when any were refused, `rulings.commentRefused(runId, 'Flow rulings refused (RULE job <id>): <item> (<reason>)[; ...]')`.

**Refused rulings are skipped, not fatal.** A ruling for an item that is not in the RULE task (a finding, a CI item, an unknown id), or one that `ruleTarget` refuses (invalid text, already applied) or whose item index is missing, is not recorded. The flow lists the refused ones in one `commentOnRulingResult` comment on the RUN's tickets (the Finish ticket; nothing is posted when no ticket exists, i.e. every ruling was refused) and in the `flow.rulings` activity record, and continues to `FIX`. If the PR stays held, the human is asked anyway.

The RUN job returned by latestJobForStep is a FleetJobRecord: its openItems are at run.workflow.openItems, not a top-level run.openItems property; the algorithm's run.openItems shorthand refers to that workflow projection. Map each verdict { item, ruling } to the single createForFlow input object's { itemId: item, text: ruling }, retaining the RUN job id, flow creator, approval request, flow/project identity and resolved project slug.

Tick crashes after recording some rulings -> the next tick re-runs; already-recorded identical rulings are returned, not duplicated. `createForFlow` owns each ruling transaction; never invoke it inside the decide transaction or a batch transaction. The final lockLive guards progress/activity/refusal comments after the ruling loop, not a rollback of per-ruling transactions already committed before a concurrent stop. Keep last duplicate occurrences in their verdict order. Reuse commentRefused rather than injecting a second ticket-effects dependency.

Create fleet-flow-advance-rulings.spec.ts using the advance-calls harness precedent. Add a final FleetPrRulingsService stub with ruledItemIds: async () => [] and createForFlow/commentRefused stubs to positional constructors in fleet-flow-advance.service.spec.ts, fleet-flow-advance-calls.spec.ts, fleet-flow-advance-review.spec.ts and fleet-flow-advance-answers.spec.ts; existing assertions are unchanged. In flows.module.spec.ts, add .overrideModule(FleetRulingsModule).useModule(StubFleetRulingsModule) beside existing overrides, with a stub exporting FleetPrRulingsService; existing assertions are unchanged and module compilation stays a no-DB unit test.

**Scope** — In: DI/module seam, apply_rulings pass, access check, unbilled execution, per-ruling task eligibility, duplicate-last behavior, progress marker, refusal reporting, activity, recovery cases and preservation of the existing apply_calls path. Out: lazy decision inputs and RULE dispatch guidance (US-011), actual ruling writer implementation (US-005), schema/repository implementation (US-006), question payload change (US-009). A new permission key or approval for the RULE step or the flow's rulings. Cancelling a running job when the flow stops.

**Interface**
```ts
// FlowStepAction work variant passed outside decide
{ kind: 'apply_rulings'; jobId: string; rulings: FlowRuling[] };
// upstream service/repository seams
createForFlow(input: FlowRulingInput): Promise<FlowRulingOutcome>;
ruledItemIds(runJobId: string): Promise<string[]>;
commentRefused(runId: string, body: string): Promise<void>;
recordRulings(flowId: string, featureId: string, jobId: string): Promise<void>;
```
